  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM CST Thu Nov 26 2026\n\nPrecip chances... Expect brief dry spell to continue through \nBlack Friday as surface low remains well off to our east, though \nassociated cloud shield should linger over the forecast area \nthrough Friday afternoon. Next chances for precip then arrive \nFriday night as weak frontal boundary and northern stream \nshortwave track through the Great Lakes. Maintain that this has \nthe appearance of a higher PoP (50 to 70% chance) low QPF \nscenario as better moisture and forcing remain off to our north in\nproximity to the low, though there should be just enough lift to \ngenerate light precip through Saturday morning. Precip type will \nbe tricky to pin down as the freezing line hovers just northwest \nof the Fox Valley, though generally expect more frozen precip \nfurther north with more rain further south. With warm layer \nextending to at least 6k ft, would rule out snow as the dominant \np-type, though cannot rule out a few flakes in the far north with \nany CAA. Transition zone from rain to freezing rain will hinge on \nsurface temperatures, as some CAMs keep surface temps above \nfreezing over central Wisconsin. Though total QPF should be held \ndown to a few hundredths of an inch, any freezing rain or drizzle \nwould be impactful, especially on untreated roads.\n\nNext bout of light precip will be late Saturday into Sunday as low\npressure ejects from the central Plains into the Ohio Valley. This\nlooks to be more of a glancing blow to our southern tier of \ncounties as better dynamics remain well off to our south and \neast, though models still attempt to develop light QPF over \nportions of central to east-central Wisconsin. P-type looks to be \nmainly rain, though the boundary layer may get cool enough to \nproduce a few flakes just north of the Fox Valley. Regardless, \nimpacts once again look to be minimal as lack of moisture fails \nto generate any substantial QPF.\n\nSeveral chances for precip exist throughout the upcoming week as\nsynoptic flow amplifies and 500 mb wave train proceeds full steam\nahead. At this time, most of our precip chances look to originate\nfrom northern stream shortwaves, which would be moisture-starved\nby nature. P-type issues also likely to come into play as 540 \nthickness line wobbles from southern Canada to the Great Lakes.\nHowever, it remains to soon in the forecast cycle to ascertain \nany details.\n\nTemperatures... Above normal temperatures continue through\nSaturday before dipping back to near normal Sunday and Monday, \nreading mainly in the 30s and 40s. Return flow will then rebound \ntemperatures to near normal around mid-week, with highs once \nagain flirting with 50 degrees for some locations.",
